          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-4fdc0eb6f1d52e8e\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.92,\"analysis\":\"Natasha directly and emphatically avows her love for Pierre. The abrupt timing, intensifier, and repetition make the delivery emotionally forceful, but they do not independently show that the words are a reflex-like discharge rather than a passionate assertion of her enduring feeling. The utterance does not itself undertake or renew a commitment.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The sudden, repeated formulation has expressive coloring, but the passage supplies no clear evidence of involuntary blurting or other independent expressive/reflexive force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Natasha directly addresses her husband Pierre: «Я ужасно люблю тебя!» ('I love you terribly!').\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Natasha has been reflecting with wonder and admiration that Pierre—whom she sees as socially important and intellectually exceptional—is also her husband. Her declaration abruptly gives voice to the loving feeling prompted by that reflection. Their established marriage, family intimacy, and Pierre's reciprocal remarks confirm sincere, enduring affection, but do not turn this particular sentence into a relational undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"«Я ужасно люблю тебя! — сказала вдруг Наташа. — Ужасно. Ужасно!»\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person present-tense declaration strongly supports truth-conditional avowal; «вдруг» and the repetition establish emotional intensity but not by themselves independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Natasha wonders how someone so important and necessary to society can also be her husband and wants to express that wonder.\",\"supports\":\"Her declaration arises from conscious admiration and reflection, favoring interpretation as a meaningful avowal rather than an unmediated verbal reflex.\",\"confidence\":0.91},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Pierre responds that he never stops loving her, and the narration presents their family life as happy and tranquil.\",\"supports\":\"The marital exchange corroborates an ordinary, sincere declaration of enduring love while providing no specific commitment enacted by Natasha's sentence.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as affirmative, present-tense, canonical explicit love and retained as VALID_EXPLICIT_LOVE_I_YOU.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted wording is a canonical affirmative love declaration rather than a negated, hypothetical, or non-love use.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Emotional intensity and abruptness are adequately represented as contextual features, without requiring E, P, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
